---
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../Section.astro";
import ProjectPubs from "../../layouts/ProjectPubs.astro";
import PubButton from "../buttons/PubButton.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import mask from "../../assets/img/mask.png";
import classLesson from "../../assets/img/class-lesson.png";
import cntl from "cntl";

const firstParagraphClasses = cntl`
  first-letter:text-8xl
  first-letter:font-semibold
  first-letter:mr-2
  first-letter:float-left
`;

const spaceClasses = cntl`mr-1`;
---

<div>
  <SectionHead
    title="AI & Computational Tools"
    subtitle="Applying machine learning and quantitative methods to paleontological and biological data"
  />
  <Content>
    <figure class="sm:float-right sm:ml-4 mb-4 mx-auto w-full max-w-[420px] sm:w-[420px] block sm:block">
      <Image
        src={mask}
        alt="Fossil horse tooth segmentation mask and PCA visualization of patch features using deep learning"
        width={420}
        class="rounded-xl"
      />
      <figcaption class="text-xs text-porsche-950 dark:text-porsche-50 mt-2 pl-3 border-l-2 border-porsche-950 dark:border-porsche-50 leading-relaxed">
        Deep learning applied to fossil horse teeth: segmentation mask (left) and PCA visualization of patch features (right).
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="sm:first-letter:text-8xl sm:first-letter:font-semibold sm:first-letter:mr-2 sm:first-letter:float-left">
      Artificial intelligence and machine learning are transforming how paleontologists can interrogate large morphological datasets. My
      research applies deep learning models — including convolutional neural networks and interpretable AI approaches — to images of
      vertebrate fossils to classify taxa, quantify morphological variation, and detect macroevolutionary patterns at scales previously
      impossible with manual measurement. A key focus of this work is understanding how sample size, taxonomic diversity, and image
      quality affect model performance, with practical implications for museum digitization and collections-based research.
    </p>

<div class="clear-right mt-8 pt-6 border-t border-porsche-950/20 dark:border-porsche-50/20">
  <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-porsche-300 mb-3">The Shark AI Project</h3>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Alongside fossil horse research, I have contributed to the <a href="https://education.ufl.edu/sharkai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">SharkAI project</a>,
        which uses fossil shark teeth as a tangible, accessible dataset for building and evaluating machine learning image
        classification models. This project bridges cutting-edge computational methods with museum collections, producing tools
        and curricula usable by both researchers and K-12 students. My work here also contributes to broader conversations about
        bias in AI training data, model interpretability, and the use of natural history collections as computational resources.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="clear-right mt-8 pt-6 border-t border-porsche-950/20 dark:border-porsche-50/20">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-porsche-300 mb-3">Chewing on Change — Module 4: AI & Machine Learning</h3>
      <p class="mt-2">
        <a href="https://github.com/stephkillingsworth/CoC_Chapter4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">Chewing on Change</a>
        is a K-12 curriculum on the macroevolution of horses in response to climate change, developed in partnership with the
        <a href="https://www.floridamuseum.ufl.edu/fossilproject/chewing-on-change-horse-evolution/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">Florida Museum of Natural History</a>.
        Module 4 introduces students to machine learning by having them use real fossil horse tooth images and a
        cloud-based Jupyter notebook on <a href="https://mybinder.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">mybinder.org</a> —
        no coding experience required. Students train and evaluate an image classification model, explore how AI identifies
        patterns in fossil data, and discuss the broader implications of AI across science disciplines through a jigsaw reading activity.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Classroom materials include a <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1KZEAHwL1NOYLkC4ItYknB9XMnzVfvBlX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">full curriculum PDF</a>,
        <a href="https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/10gKRaOWJf8B2cZB6aLbFMtzlbLO6JCk-" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">teaching slides</a>,
        a <a href="https://youtu.be/X-fqzLilK3U?si=UVowr8p4pphxGJ3E" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">video walkthrough</a>,
        and an <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1QUaz9mrAzDCPI8hBsMQYRmU5uphps6K0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">under-the-hood code explainer</a>
        to support teachers and students who want to go deeper. A curated set of readings on AI applications across disciplines
        and areas of concern — including water use, energy costs, and data center ethics — are available for classroom discussion.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        This module is part of an IRB-approved research study
        (<span class="font-semibold">UF-IRB202600133</span>: <span class="italic">Chewing on Change: Using AI to Study Fossil Horse Teeth</span>)
        investigating how student and teacher understanding of macroevolutionary traits, AI/ML literacy, attitudes about science,
        and self-efficacy for using AI change after participating in the lesson. The study is funded by the
        <span class="font-semibold">National Science Foundation</span> <a href="https://www.nsf.gov/awardsearch/showAward?AWD_ID=2147625" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-porsche-300 hover:text-porsche-200 underline underline-offset-2">(NSF2147625)</a>
        and is currently being implemented in middle and high school classrooms across
        <span class="font-semibold">Florida, Georgia, and Ohio</span>.
      </p>
      <figure class="mt-6">
        <Image
          src={classLesson}
          alt="Students participating in the Chewing on Change Module 4 AI lesson in a classroom"
          width={450}
          class="rounded-xl"
        />
        <figcaption class="text-xs text-porsche-950 dark:text-porsche-50 mt-2 pl-3 border-l-2 border-porsche-950 dark:border-porsche-50 leading-relaxed">
          Students engaging with Module 4 of the <span class="italic">Chewing on Change</span> curriculum,
          using machine learning to classify fossil horse teeth.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Content>

  <div class="mt-4 mb-2 text-semibold text-lg">Publications:</div>
  <ProjectPubs author="MacFadden et. al.">
    <PubButton
      author="MacFadden, B. J., Barberis, C., Vallejo-Pareja, M. C., Zbinden, S. P., Perez, V. J., Killingsworth, S. R., Marks, K., Hall, D., Porto, A."
      year="2025"
      title="AI and paleontology: Effects of vertebrate fossil sample size on machine learning image classification"
      doi="https://doi.org/10.1017/pab.2025.10084"
      pdfUrl="https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/paleobiology/article/ai-and-paleontology-effects-of-vertebrate-fossil-sample-size-on-machine-learning-image-classification/BC7746C52F1965B9AB2C132F2EF73DC6"
    />
  </ProjectPubs>
  <ProjectPubs author="Wusylko et. al.">
    <PubButton
      author="Wusylko, C., Still, R., Antonenko, P. D., Abramowitz, B., Perez, V., Waisome, J., Killingsworth, S., MacFadden, B."
      year="2026"
      title="Exploring Bias and Building AI Models: Data Science for Middle Schoolers"
      doi="https://doi.org/10.1080/08872376.2026.2632617"
      pdfUrl="https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08872376.2026.2632617"
    />
    <span class={spaceClasses}>,</span>
    <PubButton
      author="Wusylko, C., Antonenko, P., Abramowitz, B., Waisome, J., Perez, V., Killingsworth, S., MacFadden, B."
      year="2025"
      title="Supporting Teachers to Integrate AI and Computer Science Through SharkAI: A Middle School Computer Science and Paleontology Curriculum"
      doi="https://doi.org/10.70725/161207qresyx"
      pdfUrl="https://www.learntechlib.org/primary/p/225068/"
    />
  </ProjectPubs>
</div>